// SOS CHILD: the handle-less process (sawos design 2 D-4).
//
// THIS FILE IS THE SHAPE OF EVERY M3 CHILD, and it is deliberately three lines
// long. A child created by `process_create` and run by `start()` receives
// `NO_HANDLE` in its first argument register — the no-boot-tag form — so it
// holds no authority at all: no console, no clock, no way to end itself
// politely. `give` (unit 3) is what will hand a child its first handle, and
// until then a fully sandboxed compute process is a FEATURE rather than a state
// to detect.
//
// SO IT DIES BY TRYING. `System(boot_handle: 0)` builds a wrapper around handle
// zero, which §3 reserves to name nothing, and the first byte of the print
// below is an `ecall` the kernel answers by TERMINATING this process for a bad
// handle. That is the fault path a SYSCALL takes, which is exactly the one this
// unit had to generalize: before design 2 a fault ended the machine, and now it
// ends a process and reschedules.
//
// WHAT THE TRANSCRIPT SHOWS, and what it would show if the unit were broken:
//
//   SOS: process fault: bad handle process=0x00000001   <- process ONE
//   SOS: process teardown ... process=0x00000001        <- and the machine
//   SOS lifecycle: root survived ...                    <- ...stayed up
//
// A kernel that still stopped on any process's death would end the run at the
// second line, and root's would never be printed.
//
// It names no architecture and would build for either profile unchanged; the
// only per-machine thing about it is the linker script the manifest points at.

import sos.{System}

@export("_start")
func _start(boot_handle: UInt) {
    // The wrapper is honest: `boot_handle` really is what the kernel put in the
    // register, and for a child in this unit that is `NO_HANDLE`.
    let system = System(boot_handle: boot_handle)

    // This does not come back. The first `ecall` names handle 0, the kernel's
    // dispatch resolves it to nothing, and design 178's faults ruling ends the
    // process — which is the point of the case rather than an accident of it.
    let _ = system.debug_print("a child with no handles cannot say this\n")

    // Unreachable. A child that somehow survived the call above still has
    // nowhere to go: there is no `exit` without a Process handle, so spinning
    // inside its own granted region is the only legal thing left. The harness
    // would catch it as a timeout rather than as a pass.
    while { }
}
